// @check
// @error return type mismatch: expected boolean, got i32 | string | boolean
// THE BARE-NAME RESTRICTION, one level further out than `guard-else-path-alias-write-
// reject.vl` states it. That file says a property PATH's narrowing may not survive its
// own `if`'s else arm; this one says it may not be HANDED UP a chain either, and the
// argument is the same fact read at a different scope.
//
// `b.v` is narrowed to `i32 | boolean` on the false edge of `b.v is string`, and that
// narrowing is an `npKeys` overlay entry, not a shadowing `declare`. A call inside the
// arm — `clobber(b)` — RETIRES the entry in place, and the retirement is popped with the
// arm's scope. Re-deriving the fact in the parent would therefore resurrect exactly the
// narrowing the arm invalidated, and the resurrected view is a cast: reading a `string`
// through an `i32` slot traps.
//
// So the chain hand-up carries bare names only, and a path keeps the full declared union
// below the chain. The refusal here IS the desired verdict — the same trade
// `guard-else-path-alias-write-reject.vl` documents, and closing it properly means
// carrying an arm's RETIREMENTS out with its facts rather than widening the channel.
type Box = { v: i32 | string | boolean }
function clobber(b: Box) { b.v = "surprise" }
function f(b: Box): boolean {
  if b.v is i32 { return false }
  else if b.v is string { clobber(b) }
  return b.v
}
print(f({ v: true }))
